 disconnect between the  black rank-and-file and the black  leadership you're Jesse Jackson's you're  all Sharpton's you're in double AC PS  tend to oppose school choice because  they are siding with the unions who do  not like it not because it doesn't work  but because many of these charter  schools and many of these schools where  the vouchers would be use aren't  organized and their primary concern are  the adults in the system not the kids  until you see them fighting for laws  that have no educational justification  lastin first-out hiring policies  there's no educational justification for  that hiring teachers after two years in  the classroom giving them a job for life  making them impossible to fire  there's no educational justification for  that those are job protections and yet  you see the unions fighting for them  even to the detriment of the children  they claim to represent and then you see  these organizations like the n-double  a-c-p who take money from the unions  siding with the unions over the kids and  the parents of the kids who they claim  to represent school choice as a movement  I mean there were no charter schools in  1996 the first one in the country opened  its doors in Minnesota in 1997 now there  are tens of thousands what has to happen  for it to become the new the new normal  one of the one of the arguments that  opponents of charter schools and  vouchers make is that they can't be  scaled up to replace our public school  system that's that's a red herring they  don't need to be scaled up we don't need  a Kipp in every neighborhood in America  I live in suburban New York City we  don't need a camp up there the public  schools are just fine  right so this we know where we need  these schools we need them in our inner  cities you know in our most difficult  neighborhoods with the kids facing the  most challenges and that's where these  schools one  to be located and that's what we need we  don't need them everywhere so this  argument that oh we can't voucherize the  whole system or create charter eyes the  whole system so we can't do anything is  it is a Dodge and it should be dismissed  out of hand but where these schools are  locating they are getting results but  you do have a leadership out there from  the President on down who talks one game  but his actions do something different  so he talks a lot about school choice  but his administration is trying to shut  down a voucher program in Louisiana room  he talks a lot about school choice but  he's been trying to shut down a voucher  program in DC since the day he entered  the Oval Office the President of the  United States has never found a public  school good enough for his own children  either before he was president or since  he tells us be patient I'll fix those  public schools while his kids attend  private schools so and again he has an  old other agenda which is to continue  his support from labor from the teachers  unions from others who have a vested  interest in the status quo no matter  what no matter that that status quo is  harming harming our most vulnerable kids  I think it's appalling speaking of Obama  when he was you know when he was elected  many people both on the right and left  you start your book with a kind of  discussion of you know this really this  is like a pretty great moment where you  know finally America has like moved past  a certain stage in race relations do you  think kind of discussions of race have  gotten better or worse or are they or at  least we having more now since Obama has  become president and we should we look  forward to a period and how would we get  there where race is no longer the kind  of open wound and that it seems to be an  American political and cultural  discourse  we're not post-racial but I'd argue that  the left has no interest in being  post-racial they claim they want to be  post-racial but they really don't they  want to keep race front and center in  our national conversations because it  serves their agenda that racism is the  all-purpose explanation for what ails  black America there are a lot of people  making a lot of money on that there  and they want to keep it out there there  are political parties who who gain and  keep political power with that narrative  out there and they want to keep talking  about it by the same token I mean you  know I I mean I'm not a partisan one way  or the other I don't know that you are  but you know what is the Republican  Party doing to send a message to you  know both to black America I mean what  what they've been Romney get now what  should what should they be doing and how  responsible are they for the fact that  they're lucky if they get 5% of the the  black vote and it was you know there was  a time when the Republican Party  actually was the black part oh yeah  these things these things change over  time  loyalty to parties change over time  that's certainly true there is some  black outreach among the GOP today you  have people like Paul Ryan making an  effort Chris Christie and his reelection  bid in New Jersey when in the Camden  introduced himself did pretty well among  blacks in the past you've had people  like Jack Kemp known for his work in the  inner cities you had Richard Riordan out  in LA at Steven Goldsmith and  Indianapolis but those still tend to be  exceptions by and large serious black  outreach does not occur among Republican  candidates and I don't ascribe that to  racial animus Nick I think they're being  very pragmatic they feel they don't need  this vote to win and they feels time  spent courting a constituency they're  not likely to get a big return on its  time not spent courting someone else and  I don't think that's really going to  change until the Republican Party feels  they need the black vote to win you were  speaking about immigration earlier right  now there's a huge debate in the GOP  about the Latino vote and whether they  can continue to win elections going  forward without more of this voting room  there is no such discussion about blacks  right among the GOP right now and I  don't think you're going to get one  until they feel they need this group to  to to win elections I wish they would do  more black outreach and the black  outreach that they do is pretty much  laughable going to the n-double-a-cp  every four years to give a speech is not  black outreach those folks were probably  lost to the GOP I like what Rand Paul is  doing and going into black colleges  to get that younger generation but  that's what you need to do you need to  go into the barber shops you need to go  into the community centers the churches  introduce yourself the the habit among  Republican candidates these days is  really to just concede that vote make  symbolic thing statement so I support  school choice and maybe that will win me  a few votes you've got to do more than  that thanks we'll leave it there we've  been talking with Jason Riley he is the  author most recently of please stop  helping us how liberals make it harder  for blacks to succeed for reason TV I'm  Nick Gillespie  [Music]  you
 

